await (async (page) => {
const cfg={"candidate":{"number":"291","id":"rauser-schieber-god-existence-2015","date":"2026-10-06","title":"Randal Rauser vs Justin Schieber — Does God Exist? (2015)","label":"God and Naturalism","topicCategory":"god-theism-atheism","youtubeUrl":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Y-Ju8tGlK7g","duration":"1 hr 25 min","motion":"Does God exist?","assessmentModel":"5.6 Sol","assessmentRubric":"Slugfester Reassessment Rubric v2","sourceNote":"This assessment covers the formal opening statements, rebuttals, scheduled cross-examination and both closing statements (approximately 2:10–1:27:00). Moderator introductions and logistics, the entire audience Q&A with every dependent answer and reply, and concluding host material are excluded at the editor’s request. Neither speaker receives credit, penalties, or an inference of failing to reply from excluded material. These scores assess the formal rounds, not the complete recording. The complete English automatic captions were reviewed against a participant-published partial transcript for the opening and rebuttal rounds. A short automatic transcription with speaker labels corroborated one exchange; unclear words remain excluded from scoring and quotations. Captions and timestamps are preserved without silent correction.","scoringNote":"Scores are AI-generated estimates of argumentative performance, rather than judgments about worldview truth; the separate AI Contribution does not affect these scores.","sides":{"pro":{"name":"God exists","speaker":"Randal Rauser","color":"teal"},"con":{"name":"Naturalism better fits the evidence","speaker":"Justin Schieber","color":"coral"}},"score":{"pro":76,"con":83,"winner":"con"},"summary":"Schieber’s comparative naturalist case better connects simplicity, hiddenness, cosmic hostility, and suffering to the motion, while Rauser offers serious epistemic and moral replies.","quotes":{"pro":{"text":"one can have rational belief in god apart from evidence","context":"Rauser concludes that an immediate experience of spiritual presence can sometimes confer prima facie rationality without an inferential argument."},"con":{"text":"the massive amounts of seemingly pointless suffering in the world strongly favor naturalism over theism","context":"Schieber compares how expected apparently gratuitous human and animal suffering would be under naturalism and a perfectly good, powerful God."}},"sections":[{"sectionId":"rational-belief-and-epistemic-limits","title":"Rational belief and epistemic limits","timebox":"8:10–1:23:41","score":{"pro":78,"con":82},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-experience-can-ground-basic-theistic-belief","time":"8:10","score":79,"role":"Constructive","words":"Rauser argues that naturally immediate experiences of divine presence can confer prima facie rational belief in God, much as perception supports fallible external-world belief without inference.","critique":"Strongest feature: Rauser carefully makes a modest epistemic claim, grounding possible rationality in the phenomenology of naturalness and immediacy while preserving fallibility and exposure to later defeaters. Principal limitation: the analogy with ordinary perception remains underdeveloped because widespread calibration, intersubjective checking, and dependable environmental sensitivity are available for perception but are not comparably established for spiritual seemings. Live burden: Rauser must specify discriminating conditions that separate truth-directed divine experience from imaginative projection, culturally trained interpretation, or equally immediate incompatible religious experiences. Locked score: 79 reflects a philosophically serious route to prima facie warrant whose restrained conclusion is stronger than a proof claim, while the missing reliability constraints prevent the experiential analogy from carrying substantially greater argumentative weight.","tags":[{"type":"bias","label":"Subjective validation","url":"https://cogbias.site/biases/subjective-validation/","context":"A spiritual presence feels naturally immediate, and that phenomenology is treated as prima facie warrant for the resulting God-belief."}]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-proper-basicality-needs-nonpermissive-constraints","time":"52:13","score":82,"role":"Reply","words":"Schieber argues that proper basicality needs constraints, proposing near-universal acceptance under normal conditions to distinguish ordinary foundational beliefs from permissive imaginative convictions.","critique":"Strongest feature: Schieber identifies the central permissiveness problem and offers explicit criteria intended to explain why external-world and other-minds beliefs receive foundational standing while unusual supernatural convictions require further scrutiny. Principal limitation: universal sanction is presented tentatively and risks confusing social prevalence with epistemic reliability, especially when genuine knowledge can be specialized, historically rare, or inaccessible under otherwise normal conditions. Live burden: Schieber must defend why his three criteria track truth-conducive belief formation rather than familiarity, shared embodiment, or entrenched consensus, and show that less popular basic beliefs cannot qualify independently. Locked score: 82 recognizes a direct, consequential challenge that forces Rauser to supply principled boundaries, although the proposed boundary itself needs a stronger epistemological foundation before it can decisively exclude theistic experience.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-particularism-starts-from-natural-belief-formation","time":"1:01:35","score":76,"role":"Reply","words":"Rauser defends epistemic particularism: begin with plausible cases of naturally formed rational belief, then build standards from them instead of imposing a prior method.","critique":"Strongest feature: Rauser exposes that Schieber’s restriction is not philosophically neutral and explains a recognized alternative methodology in which considered instances help determine adequate epistemic principles. Principal limitation: starting from natural belief formation does not itself vindicate the chosen cases, because culturally conditioned or systematically misleading beliefs can also feel spontaneous and ordinary to their holders. Live burden: Rauser must explain how particularist reflection corrects biased starting judgments and why reported divine experiences deserve inclusion among credible cases before the resulting theory is used to certify them. Locked score: 76 credits a relevant methodological rebuttal that prevents universal sanction from functioning as an uncontested gate, while its circularity risk and absent error-correction account leave the original discrimination problem substantially unresolved.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-normal-functioning-qualifies-universal-sanction","time":"1:04:07","score":76,"role":"Reply","words":"Schieber answers the psychopathy case by limiting universal sanction to nearly all normally functioning thinkers and presents this boundary as plausible rather than conclusive.","critique":"Strongest feature: Schieber preserves the intended criterion against a literal unanimity objection by emphasizing normal circumstances and a large-majority pattern, while candidly treating the formulation as provisional. Principal limitation: labeling dissenting cognition abnormal can become question-begging unless normality is defined independently of agreement with the belief whose basic status is being assessed. Live burden: Schieber must provide a noncircular account of normal functioning and explain why numerical convergence supplies epistemic authorization rather than merely identifying species-typical cognition. Locked score: 76 reflects a sensible clarification that blocks Rauser’s easiest counterexample and keeps the search for constraints productive, but the response does not yet establish that its qualified sociological threshold reliably distinguishes knowledge-conducive faculties from widespread systematic error.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-psychopathy-tests-universal-sanction","time":"1:03:10","score":76,"role":"Reply","words":"Rauser invokes psychopathy to test whether exceptions in moral cognition defeat universal sanction, pressing Schieber to state how much agreement and which thinkers count.","critique":"Strongest feature: Rauser turns an abstract criterion into a concrete boundary problem, demonstrating that accepted basic moral cognition already tolerates meaningful exceptions and therefore cannot require exceptionless consensus. Principal limitation: the example addresses only a minority associated with atypical functioning, so it does not closely parallel broad, persistent religious disagreement among ordinarily functioning people across cultures and traditions. Live burden: Rauser must show that the permitted exception structure extends to divine-experience beliefs without weakening the constraint until almost any naturally felt conviction qualifies. Locked score: 76 rewards an effective demand for precision that elicits Schieber’s normality qualification, while the limited analogy means it challenges the wording of universal sanction more successfully than its underlying concern about religious permissiveness.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-rational-updating-is-not-proper-basicality","time":"1:07:19","score":82,"role":"Reply","words":"Schieber allows that prior supernatural commitments might make an uncanny experience rationally relevant, but distinguishes evidential probability updating from genuinely noninferential proper basicality.","critique":"Strongest feature: Schieber draws an important epistemic distinction that prevents a conditional concession about rational interpretation from being inflated into agreement that the resulting God-belief is foundational and noninferential. Principal limitation: his account remains compressed, leaving unclear whether the experience supplies independent evidence, merely activates existing priors, or can possess immediate warrant alongside evidential consequences. Live burden: Schieber must articulate why phenomenological immediacy cannot itself generate basic justification when prior commitments influence interpretation, rather than assuming that prior sensitivity automatically makes the process inferential. Locked score: 82 reflects precise resistance to Rauser’s attempted concession and preserves the actual dispute, although a fuller model connecting experience, priors, and justificatory structure is needed to settle whether the categories must exclude one another.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-normality-threshold-challenges-methodist-test","time":"1:04:56","score":79,"role":"Reply","words":"Rauser presses who determines normality and how much agreement suffices, arguing that Schieber’s method may encode his own plausibility judgments into supposedly neutral constraints.","critique":"Strongest feature: Rauser identifies the danger that a method-first test can conceal substantive prior commitments, making its exclusions appear principled when they actually reproduce the evaluator’s antecedent sense of plausibility. Principal limitation: exposing vagueness and possible bias in Schieber’s proposal does not supply a better operational test or demonstrate that Rauser’s preferred particularist judgments are less vulnerable to the same influence. Live burden: Rauser must convert his methodological criticism into positive, independently defensible safeguards against bizarre or incompatible basic-belief claims while explaining how reflective correction works. Locked score: 79 recognizes a probing internal critique that reaches the selection mechanism behind universal sanction, but its force remains mainly diagnostic because the permissiveness problem survives without a replacement constraint.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-closing-reductio-demands-limits","time":"1:22:29","score":85,"role":"Reply","words":"Schieber closes by arguing that an unconstrained proper-basicality account risks licensing Great Pumpkin-style beliefs, so Rauser owes principled limits even if some beliefs are properly basic.","critique":"Strongest feature: Schieber accurately locates the continuing disagreement in the absence of stated limits and uses the reductio to show the practical consequence of accepting immediacy without sufficient discrimination. Principal limitation: the colorful counterexample establishes danger only if Rauser’s account genuinely lacks defeaters, fittingness conditions, or case-sensitive constraints, possibilities Schieber does not fully investigate within the formal exchange. Live burden: Schieber must demonstrate that theistic experience and obviously fanciful beliefs are relevantly alike under Rauser’s actual epistemology rather than relying on intuitive disparity to carry the reductio. Locked score: 85 reflects a forceful closing synthesis that returns the debate to an unanswered burden and avoids denying proper basicality wholesale, though the analogy requires a more exact structural comparison for decisive refutation.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"god-as-explanation-and-prior-simplicity","title":"God as explanation and prior simplicity","timebox":"12:20–54:35","score":{"pro":74,"con":84},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-transcendent-agency-is-explanatorily-permissible","time":"12:20","score":74,"role":"Constructive","words":"Rauser argues that transcendent entities are common in philosophy and ordinary agents explain events, so transcendent personal agency cannot be excluded from explanation merely by category.","critique":"Strongest feature: Rauser separates a legitimate type of explanation from its success in any particular case and challenges a categorical ban by combining accepted transcendence with familiar agent causation. Principal limitation: showing that neither transcendence nor agency is independently disqualifying does not establish that their conjunction is coherent, predictively disciplined, or explanatorily superior for any disputed observation. Live burden: Rauser must state what evidence a transcendent agent explanation predicts, what would count against it, and why its flexibility does not merely relocate the unexplained features into divine intentions. Locked score: 74 credits a useful clearance argument against exclusion by definition, while its largely permissive conclusion contributes little affirmative support to God’s existence and leaves comparative explanatory performance untouched.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-naturalism-has-prior-simplicity-advantage","time":"24:37","score":76,"role":"Constructive","words":"Schieber argues that classical theism adds specific claims of omniscience, omnipotence, and perfect goodness beyond generic supernaturalism, giving naturalism a pre-evidential simplicity advantage.","critique":"Strongest feature: Schieber carefully compares naturalism with the specific classical theism actually debated, explaining why parity between broad naturalism and broad supernaturalism would not erase the additional attributes in Rauser’s hypothesis. Principal limitation: complexity is counted mainly through asserted content, without addressing whether divine attributes form a unified conception or whether naturalism carries comparable commitments about fundamental reality and modal structure. Live burden: Schieber must justify the relevant measure of theoretical simplicity and show that the claimed prior advantage is substantial enough to matter after evidence, rather than merely noting that one hypothesis says more. Locked score: 76 recognizes a pertinent Bayesian framing and an explicit comparative reason, but the undeveloped complexity metric keeps the claimed prior from being securely quantified or even directionally decisive.","tags":[]}},{"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-concedes-explanatory-legitimacy-only","time":"53:45","score":91,"role":"Reply","words":"Schieber agrees that God may function as an explanatory hypothesis but stresses that permissibility supplies no reason to think theism true, preserving the distinction between eligibility and success.","critique":"Strongest feature: Schieber grants exactly what Rauser established and no more, sharply distinguishing categorical explanatory admissibility from evidence that a particular divine explanation is probable or superior. Principal limitation: the reply is principally classificatory and does not assess whether Rauser’s agent analogy supplies any weak positive support, nor does it offer criteria for evaluating legitimate transcendent explanations. Live burden: Schieber must still compare theistic and naturalistic explanations where Rauser presents actual evidence, rather than treating the correct logical distinction as itself a verdict against theism. Locked score: 91 reflects exceptionally accurate burden control and a decisive prevention of argumentative overreach, with the remaining limitation arising because this move disciplines one claim instead of independently advancing the broader naturalist case.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"moral-knowledge-and-disagreement","title":"Moral knowledge and disagreement","timebox":"17:56–1:19:22","score":{"pro":79,"con":80},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-god-best-explains-moral-cognition","time":"17:56","score":77,"role":"Constructive","words":"Rauser argues that reductionism sacrifices objective moral information and Platonism leaves access mysterious, whereas a perfectly good, powerful creator has reason and ability to enable moral knowledge.","critique":"Strongest feature: Rauser presents a genuine comparative argument about moral epistemology, identifies distinct costs for reductionism and Platonism, and connects divine character to both motivation and capacity for reliable cognition. Principal limitation: theism’s fit is asserted without explaining the mechanism of divine calibration, the uneven reliability of moral judgment, or why a perfect educator would permit profound and violent disagreement. Live burden: Rauser must show that theistic design makes our actual mixture of insight, error, cultural variation, and motivated distortion more expected than naturalistic moral cognition does. Locked score: 77 credits the debate’s clearest affirmative evidential case for God and its well-structured alternatives, while unresolved empirical fit and an underspecified reliability bridge prevent the proposed personal explanation from earning a stronger comparative conclusion.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-moral-intuition-can-be-prima-facie-naturalistically","time":"54:37","score":74,"role":"Reply","words":"Schieber argues that moral intuitions may be prima facie justified like logical or grammatical intuitions even when people cannot articulate the mechanism and remain open to correction.","critique":"Strongest feature: Schieber offers a modest account compatible with fallibility, correctly observing that immediate justification need not await a complete causal theory and comparing moral recognition with familiar non-discursive cognition. Principal limitation: the analogy describes how intuitions feel and function without explaining why a naturalistic process should reliably connect evaluative appearances to objective, mind-independent moral facts. Live burden: Schieber must provide either a plausible truth-tracking story or an argument that Rauser’s theistic mechanism is equally obscure, while addressing evolutionary and cultural influences on evaluative judgment. Locked score: 74 reflects a reasonable defense of provisional justification that blocks an argument from present ignorance, but it leaves Rauser’s central comparative access challenge largely standing because redescribing the faculty does not explain its reliability.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-naturalism-still-owes-moral-intuition-mechanism","time":"1:14:18","score":84,"role":"Reply","words":"Rauser replies that naming intuition and analogizing it to rational insight expands rather than solves naturalism’s access problem, since both truth-directed capacities still need explanation.","critique":"Strongest feature: Rauser directly targets the explanatory gap in Schieber’s response and notes that adding logical intuition can multiply unexplained truth-tracking faculties instead of supplying the missing naturalistic connection. Principal limitation: demanding a detailed mechanism risks an asymmetrical standard unless Rauser also explains how divine design produces reliable moral cognition and why its observed failures fit perfect goodness. Live burden: Rauser must demonstrate a comparative advantage, not merely identify naturalistic mystery, by deriving stronger expectations about actual moral competence and disagreement from the creator’s motives and methods. Locked score: 84 recognizes a sharp rebuttal to a largely descriptive answer and keeps moral epistemology focused on reliability, while theism’s own unspecified implementation prevents the objection from completing Rauser’s explanatory inference.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-moral-disagreement-is-theistically-surprising","time":"56:20","score":79,"role":"Reply","words":"Schieber argues that extreme disagreement among sincere theists is unexpected if God seeks relationship and moral maturity, whereas fallible naturalistic cognition makes such divergence unsurprising.","critique":"Strongest feature: Schieber tests Rauser’s design hypothesis against observable variation rather than merely defending naturalism, connecting a perfectly good teacher’s purposes with an expectation of clearer moral convergence. Principal limitation: the argument does not distinguish disagreement caused by cognitive access from disagreement driven by self-interest, social pressure, incomplete reflection, or freedom to resist recognized moral demands. Live burden: Schieber must show that the residual disagreement among comparably informed and sincere agents exceeds what theism’s own account of fallibility and agency would predict. Locked score: 79 reflects a relevant evidential counterweight that makes Rauser’s access argument answer the world’s actual distribution, while alternative theistic explanations and the absence of a calibrated expected level of consensus limit its force.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-closing-answers-moral-disagreement","time":"1:18:10","score":77,"role":"Reply","words":"Rauser answers that cultures share many basic moral judgments and that human fallenness and self-justification can explain persistent disagreement despite an underlying capacity to apprehend moral truth.","critique":"Strongest feature: Rauser supplies a late, substantive reply by separating foundational consensus from disputed applications and identifying motivated self-exemption as a mechanism that can coexist with genuine moral knowledge. Principal limitation: broad agreement on elementary norms may arise through shared social needs, while invoking fallenness can accommodate almost any disagreement unless it yields independent predictions about where convergence and distortion should occur. Live burden: Rauser must show that the observed pattern, including sincere conflict among reflective believers, fits divinely furnished cognition better than common naturalistic learning and coordination pressures. Locked score: 77 credits an important response that prevents Schieber from claiming unanswered disagreement, but its selective consensus evidence and flexible sin explanation do not restore a clear theistic comparative advantage.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-mechanism-unknown-may-still-favor-theism","time":"1:15:39","score":89,"role":"Reply","words":"Schieber concedes ignorance about moral intuition’s mechanism and allows that this specific datum may favor theism, while resisting the claim that naturalistic moral access is therefore absurd.","critique":"Strongest feature: Schieber makes a disciplined concession that distinguishes uncertainty about one datum from surrendering the total comparison, thereby preserving rational updating without pretending naturalism already explains moral access. Principal limitation: he offers little affirmative account of how objective moral knowledge fits naturalism, so the concession leaves Rauser’s most important positive contrast with considerable residual force. Live burden: Schieber must estimate how much the unexplained reliability shifts the comparison and whether naturalistic accounts of cognition can reduce that shift without abandoning objective moral facts. Locked score: 89 reflects exemplary intellectual calibration and accurate containment of the concession, although its defensive precision cannot substitute for the missing naturalistic explanation that motivated Rauser’s argument.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"hiddenness-and-relationship","title":"Hiddenness and relationship","timebox":"27:13–1:25:10","score":{"pro":74,"con":84},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-implicit-relationship-can-exist-without-belief","time":"46:43","score":74,"role":"Reply","words":"Rauser argues that infants and morally responsive adults may stand in meaningful relationship with God without correct propositional belief, so conscious theistic belief is not universally necessary.","critique":"Strongest feature: Rauser challenges a crucial hiddenness premise by giving intelligible examples of relationship that precedes or bypasses explicit conceptual recognition, rather than denying nonresistant unbelief exists. Principal limitation: infant dependence and anonymous orientation do not obviously provide the reciprocal, consciously available relationship whose benefits Schieber claims a loving God would seek for capable adults. Live burden: Rauser must explain what makes the unconscious relation meaningfully interpersonal and why God would withhold clearer recognition from receptive agents when explicit reciprocity could deepen rather than replace their moral orientation. Locked score: 74 credits a relevant conceptual counterexample that broadens the category of relationship, while the analogy does not adequately answer the argument concerning conscious reciprocal access for mature nonresistant persons.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-nonresistant-unbelief-favors-naturalism","time":"27:13","score":84,"role":"Constructive","words":"Schieber argues that sincere, receptive nonbelief is unlikely if a perfectly loving God seeks conscious relationship, but readily expected if naturalism is true and no God exists.","critique":"Strongest feature: Schieber develops a clear likelihood comparison, carefully limits the evidence to nonresistant unbelief, and connects explicit belief instrumentally to the possibility of conscious reciprocal relationship. Principal limitation: the probability claim depends on a demanding account of perfect love and relationship timing, without fully considering goods served by epistemic distance or forms of relationship lacking propositional recognition. Live burden: Schieber must show that such alternative divine reasons are insufficient or independently improbable, while substantiating that genuinely receptive nonbelief occurs under conditions where revelation would not damage other relevant goods. Locked score: 84 reflects a focused and philosophically consequential evidential argument with well-preserved qualifications, although its central expectation about loving action remains contestable rather than deductively secured.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-justice-seeking-can-be-implicit-divine-relationship","time":"1:10:55","score":77,"role":"Reply","words":"Rauser proposes that an atheist pursuing justice and mercy may unknowingly relate to God through those goods, as someone can encounter a reality without possessing its correct description.","critique":"Strongest feature: Rauser gives his implicit-relationship thesis concrete moral content, explaining how orientation toward justice and mercy might constitute responsiveness to divine character despite explicit atheistic belief. Principal limitation: causal or value-based connection to God does not by itself establish interpersonal reciprocity, since the agent neither recognizes a partner nor interprets action as communication with that partner. Live burden: Rauser must identify the relational features present beyond moral alignment and explain why an all-loving God would regard this thinner connection as sufficient when conscious recognition could offer the additional moral benefits Schieber emphasizes. Locked score: 77 recognizes an inventive, relevant model that weakens the necessity of correct theological concepts, but the model remains analogically suggestive rather than a demonstrated answer to conscious hiddenness.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-unknown-reasons-require-asymmetry","time":"59:30","score":82,"role":"Reply","words":"Schieber argues that merely possible unknown reasons for permitting hiddenness are offset by possible unknown reasons for preventing it unless the theist supports a probability asymmetry.","critique":"Strongest feature: Schieber correctly explains why bare appeals to inscrutable purposes do not automatically defeat a comparative likelihood argument, since symmetrical unknown possibilities cannot privilege the theistic observation without supporting structure. Principal limitation: the reply is strongest against wholly unspecified reasons, whereas Rauser offers some concrete relational models that deserve individual probability assessment rather than dismissal as generic mystery. Live burden: Schieber must evaluate those stated possibilities and show that they remain too weak, rare, or costly to reverse the expectation generated by perfect love. Locked score: 82 reflects sound comparative discipline that keeps possibility from masquerading as likelihood, while the argument would be stronger if it separated genuinely unsupported unknowns from the particular goods and timing considerations actually proposed.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-temporary-distance-may-protect-future-relationship","time":"1:13:41","score":70,"role":"Reply","words":"Rauser suggests that immediate revelation to a trauma-shaped atheist might provoke rejection, so temporary divine distance could preserve an implicit relation and enable a better future one.","critique":"Strongest feature: Rauser offers a psychologically intelligible circumstance in which disclosure timing could matter, connecting epistemic distance to preservation of a person’s eventual freedom and receptivity rather than simple divine indifference. Principal limitation: the single hypothetical possibility lacks evidence about actual nonresistant unbelievers and assumes an omniscient God cannot reveal himself in a manner suited to the individual without provoking harmful rejection. Live burden: Rauser must generalize the proposal beyond an exceptional case and establish that delayed recognition predictably improves relationship enough to explain widespread sincere nonbelief. Locked score: 70 reflects a relevant but speculative possibility that modestly complicates Schieber’s expectation, while its narrow illustration and unsupported psychological assumptions provide little comparative evidence for theism.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-conscious-reciprocal-relationship-adds-moral-benefits","time":"1:12:27","score":84,"role":"Reply","words":"Schieber grants implicit religious traditions but argues that explicit two-way relationship with a morally perfect person supplies deeper moral understanding and formation unavailable through unconscious connection alone.","critique":"Strongest feature: Schieber answers Rauser’s broadened relationship concept by identifying a concrete lost good, namely the formative guidance and reciprocal understanding made possible through conscious engagement with a perfectly moral partner. Principal limitation: the argument does not complete the comparison between those deeper benefits and possible timing goods involving coercion, resentment, developmental readiness, or distorted reception across different persons. Live burden: Schieber must show that perfect love would ordinarily prioritize immediate conscious access and that a tailored revelation could confer the claimed benefits without undermining other valuable freedoms. Locked score: 84 reflects a strong refinement that preserves the hiddenness argument after Rauser’s examples, while the expectation about conscious relationship still requires a fuller account of competing relational goods.","tags":[]}},{"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-closing-conscious-relation-remains-withheld","time":"1:24:04","score":84,"role":"Reply","words":"Schieber closes that morally admirable aid work may be good while the person’s unrecognized connection to God remains relationally thin, leaving the best conscious relationship unexplainedly withheld.","critique":"Strongest feature: Schieber carefully avoids calling the aid work trivial and isolates the disputed deficiency in the relationship itself, preserving both moral respect and the original conscious-reciprocity premise. Principal limitation: describing the implicit relation as thin does not establish that God lacks sufficient reasons for permitting that stage, especially when Rauser has proposed temporally structured relationship development. Live burden: Schieber must connect the acknowledged relational loss to a robust probability judgment across actual nonresistant people and answer whether deferred recognition could sometimes serve their eventual flourishing. Locked score: 84 reflects a clear closing clarification that prevents Rauser’s example from changing the subject, although it reiterates the core expectation more successfully than it defeats every proposed exception.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"cosmic-hostility-and-anthropocentrism","title":"Cosmic hostility and anthropocentrism","timebox":"30:49–1:25:58","score":{"pro":69,"con":85},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-hostility-argument-assumes-anthropocentrism","time":"40:01","score":69,"role":"Reply","words":"Rauser argues that expecting a human-friendly cosmos imports anthropocentrism into theism and suggests divine transcendence and immanence could instead motivate both cosmic grandeur and intimate local detail.","critique":"Strongest feature: Rauser challenges an unstated design expectation and supplies a positive aesthetic analogy in which an excellent creator produces vast grandeur alongside habitable, intimate regions rather than maximizing human convenience. Principal limitation: the response addresses size and artistic variety much more directly than pervasive lethality, unstable environments, and the extraordinary ratio of hostile to habitable regions emphasized by Schieber. Live burden: Rauser must derive an expectation for hostility itself from independent theistic purposes and explain why grandeur requires conditions that destroy embodied life almost everywhere. Locked score: 69 reflects a relevant warning against simplistic anthropocentrism, but the analogy largely changes the feature under comparison and therefore leaves the combined hostility-plus-scale evidence insufficiently answered.","tags":[{"type":"fallacy","label":"Red herring","url":"https://logfall.com/fallacies/red-herring/","context":"The response shifts from pervasive cosmic lethality to grandeur, intimacy, and whether humans require a maximally comfortable universe."}]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-hostility-plus-scale-favors-naturalism","time":"30:49","score":80,"role":"Constructive","words":"Schieber argues that a vast cosmos overwhelmingly hostile to life is awkward if designed for embodied creatures but unsurprising if indifferent natural processes govern, so the conjunction favors naturalism.","critique":"Strongest feature: Schieber explicitly combines hostility with scale, avoids treating vastness alone as evidence, and frames the observation through comparative expectations rather than claiming divine creation is logically impossible. Principal limitation: the premise that the universe was created for humans is stronger than bare classical theism and receives limited defense, while habitable locality may suffice for purposes unrelated to universal accessibility. Live burden: Schieber must justify why a loving creator would probably make a significantly larger share traversable or benign and why grandeur, stable laws, or unknown forms of value do not offset that expectation. Locked score: 80 recognizes a vivid, disciplined likelihood argument tied to concrete cosmic features, though its anthropocentric bridge and uncalibrated design expectation restrict the inference.","tags":[]}},{"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-grandeur-does-not-answer-hostility","time":"58:17","score":87,"role":"Reply","words":"Schieber accepts that divine artistry might predict vastness but argues that an architect seeking appreciation would not make nearly every area lethal, so grandeur fails to explain hostility.","critique":"Strongest feature: Schieber grants the successful portion of Rauser’s analogy and precisely identifies its omission, showing that impressive scale and dangerous inaccessibility are separable properties requiring different explanations. Principal limitation: the house analogy continues to assume that human exploration is a central purpose of the cosmos, and it does not consider whether stable physical conditions producing life locally entail hostility elsewhere. Live burden: Schieber must support the counterfactual expectation that comparable cosmic goods were achievable with substantially less danger and that accessibility should matter under classical theism independently of human-centered creation. Locked score: 87 reflects an incisive rebuttal that exposes Rauser’s failure to address the evidential conjunction, while its own design expectations remain plausible but insufficiently grounded.","tags":[]}},{"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-closing-hostility-not-maximal-hospitality","time":"1:25:10","score":87,"role":"Reply","words":"Schieber closes by retaining his premise that the universe was created for us while denying that the argument requires maximal hospitality; overwhelming lethality, he says, remains surprising.","critique":"Strongest feature: Schieber removes an exaggerated target and narrows his expectation to a modest comparative claim, making Rauser’s anthropocentrism objection less effective because only extreme hostility needs explanation. Principal limitation: even a moderate-hospitality expectation still requires support from divine goodness and creative purpose, and the closing does not establish how much safer a law-governed life-permitting universe feasibly could be. Live burden: Schieber must connect perfect goodness to the proposed middle ground and distinguish evidentially significant excess hostility from danger that follows from regular physical processes enabling embodied agency. Locked score: 87 reflects a strategically strong qualification that preserves the original conjunction and closes an obvious escape route, while metaphysical feasibility and purpose remain unquantified.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"suffering-and-unknown-purposes","title":"Suffering and unknown purposes","timebox":"33:38–1:27:05","score":{"pro":77,"con":88},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-finite-perspective-limits-suffering-inference","time":"44:37","score":77,"role":"Reply","words":"Rauser argues that finite observers see suffering without its wider causal and narrative context, like hospital-bound observers, so they are poorly positioned to judge ultimate pointlessness.","critique":"Strongest feature: Rauser explains a genuine epistemic limitation through a clear analogy, emphasizing that judgments about pointless suffering can depend on antecedents, consequences, relationships, and goods outside an observer’s accessible frame. Principal limitation: limited knowledge alone does not show that apparently gratuitous suffering is likely under theism, and indiscriminate skepticism could also undermine ordinary moral judgments about when intervention is required. Live burden: Rauser must provide positive reason to expect enough hidden justifying connections, while preserving confidence that humans can recognize preventable horrors and moral obligations in less cosmic settings. Locked score: 77 credits a serious caution against overconfident inference from appearance to ultimate purpose, but the move weakens certainty more than it reverses Schieber’s comparative evidential direction.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-apparently-pointless-suffering-favors-naturalism","time":"33:38","score":86,"role":"Constructive","words":"Schieber grants that some suffering may secure greater goods, then argues that massive apparently pointless human and animal suffering is improbable under perfect goodness but expected under indifferent naturalism.","critique":"Strongest feature: Schieber preserves the qualification between all suffering and seemingly gratuitous suffering, includes evolutionary suffering predating humans, and uses a comparative likelihood argument rather than a deductive inconsistency claim. Principal limitation: the premise depends on judgments about available greater goods and divine alternatives that finite observers may estimate imperfectly, while naturalism’s expectation is described broadly rather than probabilistically calibrated. Live burden: Schieber must defend the reliability of our assessments across representative cases and explain why the volume, distribution, or extremity remains surprising after plausible theistic goods are considered. Locked score: 86 reflects a powerful, carefully qualified evidential case anchored in diverse suffering, though the move cannot simply assume that appearance accurately measures ultimate justification.","tags":[]}},{"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-closing-possibility-does-not-equal-likelihood","time":"1:25:54","score":90,"role":"Reply","words":"Schieber replies that possible hidden reasons only show theism can accommodate suffering; without reason to expect them, accommodation does not beat naturalism’s straightforward comparative fit.","critique":"Strongest feature: Schieber sharply distinguishes compatibility from predictive likelihood and returns Rauser’s hospital analogy to the correct comparative question, preventing mere possibility from neutralizing adverse evidence automatically. Principal limitation: the response risks understating the evidential role of background theistic commitments that could independently predict human cognitive limits or morally complex providence, even without specifying each hidden reason. Live burden: Schieber must show that those commitments do not generate a meaningful prior expectation of unseen goods and that naturalism explains the particular suffering pattern rather than simply permitting it. Locked score: 90 reflects excellent probabilistic discipline and the strongest direct answer to Rauser’s skeptical move, with only the need for fuller treatment of independently motivated theistic expectations limiting an otherwise decisive exchange.","tags":[]}}]}],"overall":{"pro":{"score":76,"strengths":["Rauser carefully separates possible rational theistic belief, explanatory legitimacy, and a positive moral-knowledge inference.","His moral epistemology argument compares reductionism, Platonism, and personal design rather than merely asserting that morality requires God.","He grants nonresistant unbelief and supplies concrete implicit-relationship models instead of denying the difficult evidence.","His finite-perspective response identifies a real limit on confident judgments that suffering lacks any wider justifying context."],"blunders":[{"text":"The grandeur analogy answers cosmic scale far more clearly than the combined evidence of scale and pervasive hostility.","links":[]},{"text":"The temporary-distance case remains a speculative possibility and is not evidence explaining widespread nonresistant unbelief.","links":[]},{"text":"His divine moral-cognition explanation leaves its own reliability mechanism and the pattern of serious disagreement underspecified.","links":[]}]},"con":{"score":83,"strengths":["Schieber consistently frames naturalism and classical theism as comparative hypotheses with different evidential expectations.","He preserves crucial qualifications involving nonresistance, conscious reciprocity, hostility plus scale, and apparently pointless suffering.","His distinction between explanatory eligibility and evidential success prevents Rauser’s permissive argument from proving too much.","His closing reply correctly distinguishes theism’s possible accommodation of suffering from a reason to expect the observed pattern."],"blunders":[{"text":"The universal-sanction test risks treating social prevalence as epistemic reliability and lacks an independent account of normality.","links":[]},{"text":"His naturalistic appeal to moral intuition initially redescribes the faculty without explaining its connection to objective moral truth.","links":[]},{"text":"The cosmic argument relies on a created-for-us expectation that receives less defense than its rhetorical force suggests.","links":[]}]}},"logicalExtension":{"pro":{"finalArgument":{"thesis":"A cumulative theistic case is strongest when divine-personal explanation predicts both fallible moral knowledge and developmentally timed relationship, rather than merely accommodating each datum separately.","premises":["Humans display substantial cross-cultural convergence on basic moral prohibitions alongside predictable distortion where self-interest, group loyalty, and limited information exert pressure.","A perfectly good creator seeking mature agency could value reliable foundational moral capacities without guaranteeing error-free application or removing opportunities for responsible correction.","Conscious divine relationship may be a developmental good whose optimal timing varies when trauma, coercive interpretation, or premature certainty would impair a person’s free response.","Immediate moral awareness and occasional religious experience can therefore be treated as defeasible personal communication whose credibility depends on coherence, fruits, and resistance to counterevidence.","This model predicts neither universal explicit belief nor arbitrary hiddenness, but a structured mixture of accessible moral knowledge, corrigibility, and person-sensitive opportunities for recognition."],"conclusion":"If that structured pattern is independently more expected under purposive divine formation than under unguided cognition, Rauser’s moral and experiential arguments jointly provide modest evidence for God."},"newArguments":[{"title":"Calibrated Moral Formation","text":"Divine moral education need not produce uniform conclusions at every stage; it could instead provide basic evaluative capacities, social feedback, and enough transparency for responsible improvement. The relevant comparison would examine where disagreement clusters. Convergence on elementary harms, greater divergence under complexity or self-interest, and correction through attentive dialogue would fit a developmental design better than random moral access. This argument gains force only if those patterns are demonstrated and shown less expected under naturalistic cultural learning."},{"title":"Relational Timing Evidence","text":"Hiddenness should be disaggregated by duration, receptivity, trauma, and subsequent change. A theistic account predicts that distance justified by relationship-sensitive timing would correlate with identifiable barriers and would often give way to forms of recognition suited to the person. Permanent sincere seeking without such opportunities would count strongly against the model. Framing the claim this way converts Rauser’s isolated possibility into a risky expectation that evidence about actual trajectories could support or undermine."},{"title":"Personal Explanation Constraints","text":"A divine-agent explanation becomes informative when constrained by the agent’s stated character. Perfect goodness, knowledge, and power should jointly predict moral communication that is sufficient for accountability, responsive to individual limitations, and compatible with noncoercive freedom. These constraints rule out many imaginable worlds that an unconstrained supernatural cause could accommodate. A character-constrained divine hypothesis thus gains explanatory content by making systematically deceptive moral cognition or wholly inaccessible relationship comparatively unlikely."}]},"con":{"finalArgument":{"thesis":"Naturalism gains its strongest comparative support when several observations converge on impersonal indifference while theistic auxiliary explanations remain unconstrained and independently unsupported.","premises":["Classical theism predicts that perfect love and goodness will shape opportunities for conscious relationship, moral learning, creaturely welfare, and the intelligibility of preventable suffering.","The observed world contains sincere nonresistant unbelief, severe moral disagreement, vast lethal environments, and immense suffering with no discernible role in agency or formation.","Each observation is broadly unsurprising if conscious life emerges within indifferent physical processes whose operation is not directed toward relationship, safety, or moral instruction.","Theistic replies invoke implicit relationship, developmental delay, aesthetic grandeur, and hidden goods, but these explanations presently lack independent frequency estimates and discriminating predictions.","When one flexible hypothesis needs a different unverified purpose for each adverse observation, their combined accommodation supplies less confirmation than a hypothesis expecting indifference across all of them."],"conclusion":"The convergence of relational, moral, cosmic, and suffering evidence therefore favors naturalism unless theism can derive a unified, independently testable account of why this particular pattern should occur."},"newArguments":[{"title":"Independent Evidence Convergence","text":"Hiddenness, hostility, and suffering should not be treated as repetitions of one complaint. They arise in different domains and depend on different observations, yet each points toward the absence of purposive concern. If their evidential errors are substantially independent, their joint force can exceed any single argument even when each likelihood ratio is modest. The naturalist case should therefore defend independence explicitly and avoid double-counting features, such as suffering caused by the same hostile environment."},{"title":"Auxiliary Cost Accounting","text":"Every theistic reply adds a purpose: implicit relationship for unbelief, grandeur for cosmic scale, developmental delay for disclosure, and inaccessible goods for suffering. These proposals may each be possible, but collectively they reduce predictive sharpness unless derived from one prior theory of divine action. A disciplined comparison should charge complexity for auxiliaries introduced only after contrary evidence appears. Naturalism gains support when it accommodates the observations without comparable adjustments, while independently motivated theistic purposes would avoid that penalty."},{"title":"Distribution Over Existence","text":"The debate often asks whether suffering or hiddenness can exist under theism, but distribution is more diagnostic than bare existence. Duration, intensity, victim vulnerability, geographic accident, and the availability of human prevention provide finer evidence. Naturalism predicts strong dependence on causal exposure and power, whereas perfect providence may predict targeted limits or compensating access. Comparing those distributions can turn vague appeals to apparent pointlessness into a clearer empirical burden while leaving room for justified suffering in particular cases."}]}}},"origin":"http://127.0.0.1:4191","prefix":"debate-291-local","screenshots":true,"screenshotDirectory":"output/playwright","requiredReaderDisclosure":"This assessment covers the formal opening statements, rebuttals, scheduled cross-examination and both closing statements (approximately 2:10–1:27:00). Moderator introductions and logistics, the entire audience Q&A with every dependent answer and reply, and concluding host material are excluded at the editor’s request. Neither speaker receives credit, penalties, or an inference of failing to reply from excluded material. These scores assess the formal rounds, not the complete recording.","scopeCases":[{"id":"licona-carrier-resurrection-formal-rounds-2004","number":"255","notice":"Only the formal debate rounds (5:10–1:38:23) are assessed. The subsequent question-and-answer session, including moderator-authored challenges and both speakers' replies, is excluded; neither speaker receives credit or penalties for that material. These scores are not an assessment of the complete recording."},{"id":"aron-ra-jones-christianity-dangerous-formal-rounds-2019","number":"256","notice":"Only the opening statements and cross-examination (1:59–1:27:22) are assessed. All subsequent audience questions and answers, and both speakers' later closing statements, are excluded; neither speaker receives credit or penalties for that material. These scores are not an assessment of the complete recording."},{"id":"atkins-law-science-philosophy-formal-rounds-2014","number":"257","notice":"Only the opening statements and first rebuttals (3:01–28:50) are assessed. The entire subsequent audience-question portion, including both speakers' later replies, concessions, and concluding comments, is excluded; neither speaker receives credit or penalties for that material. These scores are not an assessment of the complete recording."},{"id":"white-price-bible-truth-formal-rounds-2010","number":"258","notice":"Only the formal rounds (7:04–2:16:31), including openings, rebuttals, cross-examinations, and both closing statements, are assessed. Introductions, all later audience questions and answers, and the moderator's separate concluding argument are excluded; neither speaker receives credit or penalties for that material. These scores are not an assessment of the complete recording."},{"id":"sharpton-hitchens-god-religion-morality-2007","number":"261","notice":"This assessment uses the complete automatic English captions and audio verification of all 31 selected arguments across 23 passages. Under the primary-speaker exception, moderator Jacob Weisberg’s substantive civil-rights, deism, and scripture interventions, together with their directly dependent replies, are excluded from both speakers’ scores."},{"id":"dembski-hitchens-good-god-existence-2010","number":"263","notice":"This assessment uses the complete automatic captions and 13 bounded, independent caption-blind audio transcription checks. It involved no direct listening and no complete-audio verification. Quotations reproduce exact eligible transcript spans; argument descriptions and critiques are condensed from the source. Advocacy by Dan Panetti and Caleb, the unnamed moderator, and Larry Taylor was excluded, totaling about 4 minutes 58 seconds, or 3.45% of the assessed window. Neither side received credit or penalty for excluded participation or for replies dependent on it."},{"id":"chopra-dawkins-consciousness-purpose-spirituality-2013","number":"264","notice":"Direct quotations reproduce exact eligible substrings from the retained timestamped English captions; argument descriptions are condensed summaries of those captions. The retained scope was reviewed through the complete English caption record. Limited independent audio transcription covered 15 bounded verification clips and two source-context clips. No direct listening or complete-recording audio verification is claimed, and the clips establish wording and attribution rather than scientific or historical truth. This assessment covers the independent exchanges between Deepak Chopra and Richard Dawkins. By explicit editorial approval, Andrés Roemer’s substantive interventions and their directly dependent replies are excluded, including the moderator-led persuasion and free-will exchange. Neither debater receives credit or penalty for the excluded material; this is not an assessment of every contribution in the recording."},{"id":"dillahunty-giunta-faith-rational-justification-2018","number":"266","notice":"By explicit editorial approval, this assessment covers only the main conversation, approximately 05:29–1:16:07, not the full 2:28:59 recording or its audience question period. Both speakers agree that faith understood as confidence can be rational; the scored question concerns their narrower disagreement over whether God’s possibility must first be demonstrated. The moderator’s substantive interventions and their directly dependent replies, including the closing exchange from approximately 1:06:18, are excluded under the primary-speaker scope rule. Neither debater receives credit or penalty for excluded material. Complete English captions were reviewed for source screening; limited independent audio transcription checked the scope boundaries and speaker changes. No direct listening or complete-recording audio verification is claimed."},{"id":"hitchens-wilson-truth-goodness-beauty-2008","number":"267","notice":"This assessment covers only the opening presentations and direct exchange, approximately 11:46–1:13:22, with the final complete reply included. Audience Q&A, the later closing statements, and the introduction are not assessed. A verified replay at approximately 59:26–1:09:10 is counted only once; timestamps refer to the original upload. Scores describe this limited excerpt, not the complete event."},{"id":"sacks-dawkins-science-religion-2012","number":"268","notice":"This assessment covers selected, self-contained Jonathan Sacks–Richard Dawkins exchanges within the main conversation, approximately 2:09–41:54. Substantive moderator interventions and their dependent replies are excluded, removing approximately 20:31 (51.6%) of that window and leaving approximately 19:14 of eligible conversation. The exclusions include host-led exchanges about book tone, selective evidence, divine intervention, religious upbringing, antisemitism, and moral necessity. Introductions and all audience Q&A are also unscored. Neither speaker receives credit or penalty for excluded material; the scores do not assess the complete event."},{"id":"craig-pigliucci-christian-god-2001","number":"271","notice":"This assessment covers both opening statements, both rounds of rebuttal, and both closing statements (approximately 6:31–1:39:05). The moderator’s subsequent third-position interlude and the entire audience Q&A, including both speakers’ answers, are excluded. Neither side receives credit or penalty for excluded material; these scores assess the approved formal debate, not the complete event."},{"id":"craig-dacey-god-existence-2004","number":"273","notice":"This assessment covers both opening statements, both rounds of rebuttal, and both closing statements (approximately 5:00–1:40:07). The entire audience Q&A, including both speakers’ answers and dependent replies, is excluded. Neither side receives credit or penalty for excluded material; these scores assess the approved formal debate, not the complete event."},{"id":"shermer-scrivener-good-without-god-2024","number":"277","notice":"By explicit editorial approval, this assessment scores only Michael Shermer and Glen Scrivener across the complete substantive exchange. Moderator Vince Vitale supplies substantive philosophical questions and challenges beyond the ordinary incidental-host allowance; his contributions remain context, and the debaters’ own replies remain eligible for assessment. No argument or score is assigned to a debater merely because Vitale advances it. Unequal questioning is considered when selecting moves and evaluating opportunities to respond. This is a recording-specific exception, not an assessment of a strictly two-speaker recording."},{"id":"lennox-tooley-atheism-christianity-2013","number":"278","notice":"The available recording ends after Michael Tooley’s closing remarks. A contemporary account also describes a closing statement by John Lennox, which is absent from the versions located. This assessment covers the available recording; neither speaker is penalized for material or replies missing from it."},{"id":"slick-barker-god-existence-2014","number":"279","notice":"This assessment uses the recoverable portions of the recording. A 33-second passage at the start of Matt Slick’s rebuttal (41:07.52–41:40.64) could not be reliably transcribed and is excluded. Neither speaker receives credit or a penalty for that passage, and no claim that an argument went unanswered relies on the missing material."},{"id":"turek-normark-god-existence-2014","number":"280","notice":"By explicit editorial approval, this assessment scores only Frank Turek and Dennis Nørmark across the substantive debate. Moderator Adam Holm contributes independent arguments and challenges, which remain context; the debaters’ own replies remain eligible for assessment. No moderator argument is credited to either debater. Unequal questioning is considered when selecting moves and evaluating opportunities to respond. This is a recording-specific exception to the ordinary two-speaker source rule."},{"id":"butt-barker-biblical-god-2009","number":"285","notice":"This assessment covers the formal opening statements, rebuttals, and cross-examination (approximately 5:54–1:08:57), plus both closing statements (1:46:09–1:57:06). The entire audience Q&A, including both speakers’ answers and dependent replies, is excluded at the editor’s request. Neither speaker receives credit or penalty for excluded material. These scores assess the formal rounds and closings, not the complete event."},{"id":"nesan-dillahunty-jesus-prophecy-2021","number":"286","notice":"This assessment covers the formal opening statements, rebuttals, cross-examination, and the speakers’ subsequent discussion before audience Q&A (approximately 0:12–1:02:31). This recording has no separate formal closing statements. The entire audience Q&A, including both speakers’ answers and dependent replies, plus the host’s after-show, is excluded at the editor’s request. Neither speaker receives credit or penalty for excluded material. These scores assess the debate portion, not the complete recording."},{"id":"johnson-barker-god-existence-2022","number":"287","notice":"This assessment covers both formal opening statements and rebuttals (approximately 10:28–1:03:56), plus both closing statements (1:22:28–1:30:03). The entire audience Q&A, including both speakers’ answers and dependent replies, and the hosts’ introductory and concluding discussion are excluded at the editor’s request. Neither speaker receives credit or penalty for excluded material. These scores assess the formal rounds and closings, not the complete recording."},{"id":"shermer-vandeweghe-jesus-miracles-2019","number":"288","notice":"This assessment covers both formal opening statements and rebuttals (approximately 6:56–1:11:59), plus both closing statements (1:49:07–1:58:23). The entire moderated discussion and audience Q&A, including both speakers’ answers and dependent replies, and the introductory montage and surrounding host remarks are excluded at the editor’s request. Neither speaker receives credit or penalty for excluded material. These scores assess the formal rounds and closings, not the complete recording."},{"id":"wielenberg-johnson-objective-morality-2020","number":"289","notice":"This assessment covers only the debaters’ direct exchange (approximately 33:05–1:13:25), as approved by the editor. The introductory position discussion, audience questions and all dependent answers, including the final reply to an audience question, are excluded. This recording has no separate formal opening speeches or paired closing statements. Neither speaker receives credit, penalties, or an inference of failing to reply from excluded material. These scores assess the direct exchange, not the complete recording."},{"id":"rauser-malpass-evidential-problem-evil-2021","number":"290","notice":"This assessment covers Alex Malpass’s opening and the debaters’ direct exchange (approximately 6:33–1:06:47), as approved by the editor. Host introductions and interruptions, audience questions and every dependent answer, and the host-only remainder are excluded. This recording has no paired formal rounds or closing speeches. Neither speaker receives credit, penalties, or an inference of failing to reply from excluded material. These scores assess the approved opening and exchange, not the complete recording."},{"id":"rauser-schieber-god-existence-2015","number":"291","notice":"This assessment covers the formal opening statements, rebuttals, scheduled cross-examination and both closing statements (approximately 2:10–1:27:00). Moderator introductions and logistics, the entire audience Q&A with every dependent answer and reply, and concluding host material are excluded at the editor’s request. Neither speaker receives credit, penalties, or an inference of failing to reply from excluded material. These scores assess the formal rounds, not the complete recording."}],"year":"2015","videoId":"Y-Ju8tGlK7g","modelLabel":"5.6 Sol","tagCount":2,"people":[{"name":"Randal Rauser","slug":"randal-rauser"},{"name":"Justin Schieber","slug":"justin-schieber"}],"graph":{"sectionSideScores":3206,"minimum":48,"maximum":95,"buckets":[{"range":"48–49","count":1},{"range":"50–51","count":1},{"range":"52–53","count":1},{"range":"54–55","count":1},{"range":"56–57","count":3},{"range":"58–59","count":9},{"range":"60–61"